@stevehou It shows a disrespect to your readers’ time (coming from someone who’s writing can be too verbose).

For instance, the length of substack posts have really blown out this year…as a reader, it is not an improvement

@LukeGromen @stevehou Yes...On this front, I try (and fail) often. But, I do keep my reader's time in mind whenever I write. In the finance world, great writing is maximally efficient.

I'm getting novella-length substacks in my inbox regularly now. Lazy and amateurish to think longer = better.

@stevehou Everyone has to decide where to draw that line. I have continued writing everything as I always did. The only thing I let AI edit is my conclusions.

@17thStCap @stevehou If you're just going to let AI speak for you, then what is the point...

First of all, this is likely wrong. I've seen readings of "100%" for my own work when the correct percentage was ~30%.
Second, who cares? The ideas and experience are clearly his. If Claude makes the difference between meeting a deadline for publication Tuesday, instead of Wednesday, the world is far better off.

@jameswester @hosseeb @pangram I thought Druck's piece was clear, hardly "bad" writing, maybe just not how you like it.

